For once I have to agree with Henry: why is a wizard's spellcasting 
restricted in a way that no other class's spellcasting is? If 
spellcasting should be restricted in wizards who wear armor, why is it 
not restricted in nonwizards who wear armor?

I admit there is /some/ precedent for this in RPG systems. I seem to 
recall that in MERP (Middle-Earth Role Playing) there were two types of 
magic - "essence" and "channelling" which were "wizardly" and "priestly" 
magic - and that one was more lenient on armour than the other.

But when the same spell is cast by two characters with the same Int, 
Wis, XL, skill and armor, why is the nonwizard better?

It doesn't strike me as intuitive or internally consistent, though it 
may be right from a balance perspective.

Also, do monks get the same body-armor penalty for spellcasting?

